---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import { RealGdpLogScale } from '../components/islands/RealGdpLogScale'
import { GrowthAndShadow } from '../components/islands/GrowthAndShadow'
import { economy, income, vintageOf } from '../data'

const sections = [
  { id: 'one-picture', label: 'One picture' },
  { id: 'growth-shadow', label: 'Growth and its shadow' },
  { id: 'who-works', label: 'Who works' },
  { id: 'prices-rates', label: 'Prices and rates' },
  { id: 'labor-capital', label: 'Labor and capital' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The economy | US federal finance"
  description="Real growth, work, prices and the split between labor and capital, from 1950."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 1 of 3</p>
  <h1>The economy</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    The denominators everything else on this site is measured against, and one thing they do
    not show on their own.
  </p>
  <section id="one-picture">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Seventy-five years, ten times the output</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Real GDP was $2.38 trillion in fiscal 1950 and $23.72 trillion in fiscal 2025, both in
      FY2025 chained dollars. That is 9.95 times as much output, an average of 3.1% a year over
      75 years.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Real GDP grew 895% between FY1950 and FY2025, from $2.383T to $23.718T in FY2025 chained
      dollars.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Real GDP grew 895% between fiscal 1950 and fiscal 2025, from $2.38 trillion to $23.72 trillion in FY2025 chained dollars, tracing a near-straight line on a log scale with visible bends at the 2008 to 2009 recession and in fiscal 2020."
      title="Real GDP, fiscal 1950 to 2036, log scale"
      source={economy._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(economy._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="real GDP in $ trillions, FY2025 chained, on a base-10 log scale"
      note="The series continues to FY2036 as a CBO baseline projection. Projected years are drawn as a separate dashed line beyond the marked FY2025 boundary and are not actuals."
    >
      <RealGdpLogScale
        client:visible
        rows={economy.data}
        lastActualFy={economy._meta.estimate_boundary!.last_actual_fy}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The vertical axis is a base-10 log scale. Equal distances up the axis are equal percentage
      changes, not equal dollar amounts, so a constant growth rate draws as a straight line. On a
      linear axis the last twenty years would tower over the first fifty and every earlier
      deviation would flatten into the baseline. Here the 2008 to 2009 recession and the fiscal
      2020 dip read as bends in a line whose slope is otherwise close to constant.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Fiscal years, not calendar years. FY2025 ran October 2024 to September 2025. The CBO file
      runs to FY2036, and everything after FY2025 is a baseline projection, drawn as a dashed
      line beyond the marked boundary. Nominal GDP in FY1995 was $7.56 trillion, the same
      denominator the government route divides by.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="growth-shadow">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 2</span></p>
    <h2>Output per hour, and what a household got</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Between 1984 and 2024, output per hour in the nonfarm business sector rose 116.5%. Real
      median household income rose 38.6%. Both are indexed to 1984 = 100.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Indexed to 1984 = 100, output per hour reached 216.5 by 2024 and real median household
      income reached 138.6.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Indexed to 1984 = 100, output per hour reached 216.5 by 2024 while real median household income reached 138.6, the two lines separating over the period."
      title="Output per hour and real median household income, 1984 to 2024, indexed"
      source={`Output per hour: ${economy._meta.source}. Median household income: ${income._meta.source}`}
      vintage={vintageOf(economy._meta)}
      xUnit="Year (fiscal for output per hour, calendar for median household income)"
      yUnit="index, 1984 = 100"
      note="Two sources on two calendars. Output per hour is CBO, fiscal years; real median household income is Census via FRED, calendar years in 2024 dollars. The two use different price indexes and different units of observation, so the vertical gap between the indexed lines is not itself a measurable quantity."
    >
      <GrowthAndShadow client:visible economyRows={economy.data} incomeRows={income.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      The two lines are not directly commensurable and the gap between them is not a quantity of
      anything. Output per hour is real nonfarm business output divided by hours worked, published
      by CBO on a fiscal year basis. Real median household income is a Census Bureau household
      survey median, published on a calendar year basis in 2024 dollars. They come from different
      sources, are deflated by different price indexes, and count different units of observation:
      one is per hour worked, the other is per household.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The shared window is 1984 to 2024. Median household income does not start until 1984 and
      does not yet run past 2024, so the chart is drawn over that window rather than over the full
      1950 to 2025 span of the output per hour series. The table below carries the full span, with
      no data marked where the Census series does not reach.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      This chart shows that the two series diverged. It does not show why, and nothing here
      identifies a cause.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.slice(2).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
